.TH PORTCONF 1 "2026-08-03" "portconf 2\.0\.6_pre1" "Gentoo Utilities"
.SH NAME
portconf \- /etc/portage configuration cleaner and manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B portconf
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B portconf
automatically detects and cleans up configuration issues in
.IR /etc/portage .
It handles USE flags, keywords, package atoms, backups, overlays, and the world file.
.PP
Operations that modify
.I /etc/portage
automatically create a timestamped backup beforehand when apply mode is enabled.
Up to
.B COUNT
(default 10) backups are retained.
.PP
The default action mode is a dry-run: planned changes are displayed but no
persistent state, including backup archives, is written.  Use
.B \-\-ask
to apply interactively or
.B \-\-force
to apply without prompting.
.PP
Destructive multi-step operations use same-filesystem staging and an undo
journal.  Ordinary failures roll back world regeneration, layout conversions,
restores, and repository cleanup before the command returns.  Backup archives
are verified before publication and rotation.  Restore archives are rejected
unless all members remain inside the expected target root and use supported
file types.
.PP
Applying runs hold an exclusive advisory lock at
.IR /var/lib/portconf/.portconf.lock .
If another applying process owns the lock, portconf exits without changing
state.  Dry-runs and read-only queries do not acquire it.
.PP
Rewritten files preserve their existing access metadata.  File/directory
layout conversions map permissions and ownership onto the new representation
instead of resetting every generated file to mode 0644.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Common
.TP
.B \-\-ask
Apply changes with interactive confirmations.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Apply all changes without prompting.  For restore operations, select the
newest available backup.
.TP
.BR \-rc ", " \-\-regen\-cache
Create and use a temporary eix cache, and fix overlay issues.
Highly recommended when running USE or trash operations.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-backup
Create a backup of the current
.I /etc/portage
state.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-restore
Roll
.I /etc/portage
back to a previously saved state.  In ask mode, select a backup interactively;
in force mode, restore the newest backup.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-sort
Sort and deduplicate USE flags, keywords, all
.I package.*
files, and profile/use.mask entries.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Compatibility alias for
.BR \-\-force .
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pretend
Dry-run: report planned changes without writing persistent state.  This is the
default and takes precedence over apply flags when explicitly supplied.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage summary.
.SS USE flags
.TP
.BR \-us ", " \-\-use\-sort
Sort USE flags; remove duplicates, preserving the last defined state (on/off).
.TP
.BR \-ui ", " \-\-use\-invalid
Remove invalid or redundantly global-defined USE flags.
.TP
.BR \-um ", " \-\-use\-make
Remove profile-defined USE flags from
.IR make.conf .
.TP
.BR \-sup ", " \-\-stupid\-use\-profile
Remove incorrect or duplicate masked/unmasked USE flags.
.TP
.BR \-uf ", " \-\-use\-full
Equivalent to
.BR "\-us \-ui \-um \-sup" .
.SS Keywords
.TP
.BR \-ku ", " \-\-keyword\-uniq
Sort and deduplicate
.I package.keywords
entries.
.TP
.BR \-ko ", " \-\-keyword\-one
Keep only the latest defined keyword per atom.
.SS Trash
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-trash
Remove not-found/not-installed atoms, empty files, backup files, and unused configs.
.TP
.BR \-ft ", " \-\-force\-trash
Remove all not-installed atoms (excludes
.IR package.mask ).
.TP
.BR \-sm ", " \-\-stupid\-mask
Remove incorrect masked atoms.
.TP
.BR \-sum ", " \-\-stupid\-unmask
Remove incorrect unmasked atoms.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-rm\-comments
Delete lines starting with
.BR # .
.TP
.BR \-ac ", " \-\-rm\-all\-comments
Delete all comments, including inline ones.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-full
Equivalent to
.BR "\-s \-t \-uf \-ku \-sm \-sum" .
.SS Converting
.TP
.BR \-f2d ", " \-\-files\-2\-dirs
Convert
.I package.*
flat files to per-package directories (comments removed).
.TP
.BR \-d2f ", " \-\-dirs\-2\-files
Convert
.I package.*
per-package directories back to flat files.
.SS Profile
.TP
.BR \-apu ", " \-\-all\-profiles\-use
List USE flags defined across all profiles.
.TP
.BR \-pu ", " \-\-profiles\-use
List USE flags of the current profile tree.
.TP
.BR \-cpu ", " \-\-current\-profile\-use
List USE flags of the active profile.
.SS World
.TP
.BR \-wb ", " \-\-world\-backup
Back up the current world file.
.TP
.BR \-wr ", " \-\-world\-restore
Roll the world file back to a saved state.
.TP
.BR \-wg ", " \-\-world\-regen
Regenerate (clean up) the world file; backup is automatic.
.SS Overlays
.TP
.BR \-fr ", " \-\-fix\-repos
Remove unused overlays and stale dependency cache entries.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration is read from
.I /etc/portconf.conf
if it exists.  Available settings:
.TP
.B COUNT
Number of backups to retain (default: 10).
.TP
.B IGNORE_CATEGORY
Space-separated list of categories to skip (shell glob patterns accepted).
.TP
.B IGNORE_PN
Space-separated list of package names to skip.
.TP
.B PORTCONF_DEFAULT_OPTS
Default options prepended to every invocation (default:
.BR \-rc ).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/portconf.conf
Site-wide configuration file.
.TP
.I /etc/portage/
Portage configuration directory managed by portconf.
.TP
.I /var/lib/portconf/
Directory where timestamped backups are stored.
.SH DEPENDENCIES
.B portconf
requires
.BR bash (1),
.BR flock (1),
.BR eix (1),
.BR qlist (1),
.BR qatom (1),
.BR versionsort (1),
.BR emerge (1),
.BR eselect (1),
and
.BR agrep (1).
.PP
.BR qlist (1),
.BR qatom (1),
and
.BR versionsort (1)
are all part of
.BR app-portage/portage-utils .
.BR flock (1)
is provided by
.BR sys-apps/util-linux .
.BR agrep (1)
is provided by
.B dev-libs/tre
and is only required by the
.B \-\-use\-invalid
and
.B \-\-use\-full
operations.
